{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And strip Aaron of his garments</b> – These are the garments of the High Priesthood, with which his son Eleazar was now invested. The plain sense is likely that when Aaron had finished performing the daily offering, burned the incense, and kindled the lamps, he brought him up Mount Hor while he was still dressed in the priestly garments and stripped them off him. On the plain level he dressed him in the shrouds of the dead that he had prepared for him, and he stripped Eleazar of his weekday garments and dressed him in the sacred garments, just as he had done with him on the day of ordination (Leviticus 8:7–9). And the midrash of our Sages is that miracles were performed with them, for they said: How could Moses strip Aaron in proper order — are not the upper garments always upper and the lower garments always lower? Rather, the Omnipresent performed for him a miracle of the garments at his death greater than in his lifetime. Moses stood him against the rock and stripped him of the priestly garments, and the garments of the Shekhinah were donned beneath them. \"And he dressed Eleazar his son with them\" (Numbers 20:28) — but how could he dress the garments on Eleazar in proper order? Rather, great honor did the Omnipresent apportion to Aaron at his death greater than in his lifetime: that the garments of the Shekhinah were donned first from below upward, and then Moses turned back and stripped Aaron of the garments in order and dressed Eleazar in order. Thus it is taught in Sifrei Zuta (Melu'im 1:6)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "I read 'ת״כ' as Sifrei Zuta based on the citation form and reference; the manuscript abbreviation could conceivably resolve differently."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:22.585154941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}